The C program's whole output is a raw memory dump of a struct, so the translation hinges on two things:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Struct layout — [38;5;10mhouse_t { int; int; double }[0m is 16 bytes on x86-64 SysV with fields at offsets 0, 4, 8 and no padding. I confirmed this against the compiled C binary rather than assuming it, and modelled the struct as an explicit zeroed [38;5;10m[u8; 16][0m with little-endian field writes. That keeps it safe Rust (no [38;5;10mtransmute[0m or pointer casts) while guaranteeing the same hex dump. [38;5;10mbathrooms = 2.0[0m is [38;5;10m0x4000000000000000[0m, which is why every output ends in [38;5;10m...0040[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mscanf("%d", &x)[0m — implemented byte-at-a-time over locked stdin, so it skips whitespace across newlines like the C runtime does. On matching failure or EOF it assigns nothing and [38;5;10mx[0m stays [38;5;10m0[0m, matching the C. For overflow I probed glibc's actual behavior: it saturates at [38;5;10mLONG_MAX[0m/[38;5;10mLONG_MIN[0m and stores the truncated low 32 bits, so [38;5;10m99999999999999999999[0m yields [38;5;10mffffffff[0m and the negative equivalent yields [38;5;10m00000000[0m. The Rust saturates in [38;5;10mi64[0m then casts with [38;5;10mas i32[0m to reproduce that, including the mid-range case ([38;5;10m5000000000[0m → [38;5;10m00f2052a[0m).[0m[0m
